WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
Let's say you're building a machine learning system and you're trying to decide whether

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.940
or not to use an end-to-end approach.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.040
Let's take a look at some of the pros and cons of end-to-end deep learning so that

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.600
you can come away with some guidelines on whether or not an end-to-end approach seems

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.160
promising for your application.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.960
Here are some of the benefits of applying end-to-end learning.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.800
First is that end-to-end learning really just lets the data speak.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.800
So if you have enough x, y data, then whatever is the most appropriate function

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.400
mapping from x to y, if you train a big enough neural network, hopefully the neural

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.800
network will figure it out.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.440
And by having a pure machine learning approach, your neural network learning

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.800
and input from x to y may be more able to capture whatever statistics are in the

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.920
data rather than being forced to reflect human preconceptions.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.720
So for example, in the case of speech recognition, earlier speech systems had

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:03.400
this notion of a phoneme, which was a basic units of sound like k, a, and t for the

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.560
word cat.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.920
And I think that phonemes are an artifact created by human linguists.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.560
I actually think that phonemes are a fantasy of linguists that are a

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.200
reasonable description of language, but it's not obvious that you want to force

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.080
your learning algorithm to think in phonemes.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.880
And if you let your learning algorithm learn whatever representation it wants to

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.640
learn rather than forcing your learning algorithm to use phonemes as a

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.840
representation, then its overall performance might end up being better.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.160
The second benefit of end-to-end deep learning is that there's less hand

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.800
designing of components needed.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.840
And so this could also simplify your design workflow, that you just don't need

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.760
to spend a lot of time hand designing features, hand designing these

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.240
intermediate representations.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.800
So how about the disadvantages?

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.600
Here are some of the cons.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.200
First, it may need a large amount of data.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:04.000
So to learn this X to Y mapping directly, you might need a lot of data of X, Y.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.120
And we've seen in a previous video some examples of where you could obtain a

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:14.560
lot of data for subtasks, such as for face recognition, where you could find

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.080
a lot of data for finding a face in the image, as well as identifying the

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.640
face once you found the face.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.760
But there was just less data available for the entire end-to-end task.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.560
So X, this is the input end of the end-to-end learning, and Y is the

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.840
output end.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.640
And so you need a lot of data, X, Y, with both the input end and the

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.560
output end in order to train some of these systems.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.360
And this is why we call it end-to-end learning, by the way, as well.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.440
Because you're learning a direct mapping from one end of the system,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.640
all the way to the other end of the system.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.080
The other disadvantage is that it excludes potentially useful hand-designed

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.040
components.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.000
So machine learning researchers tend to speak disparagingly of hand-designing

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.920
things, but if you don't have a lot of data, then your learning algorithm

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.160
doesn't have that much insight it can gain from your data if your

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.560
training site is small.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.000
And so hand-designing a component can really be a way for you to inject

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.440
manual knowledge into the algorithm.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.120
And that's not always a bad thing.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.240
I think of a learning algorithm as having two main sources of knowledge.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.720
One is the data, and the other is whatever you hand-design, be it

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.240
components or features or other things.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.720
And so when you have a ton of data, it's less important to hand-design

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.720
things.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.520
But when you don't have much data, then having a carefully hand-designed

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.760
system can actually allow humans to inject a lot of knowledge about a

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.160
problem into an algorithm, and that can actually be very helpful.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.640
So one of the downsides of end-to-end deep learning is that it excludes

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.160
potentially useful hand-designed components.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.120
And hand-designed components could be very helpful if well-designed.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.720
They could also be harmful if it really limits your performance,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.760
such as if you force an algorithm to think in phonemes when maybe

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.600
it could have discovered a better representation by itself.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.280
So it's kind of a double-edged sword that could hurt or help, but it does tend to help

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.280
more.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.600
Hand-designed components tend to help more when you're training on a small training

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:26.600
set.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.120
So if you're building a new machine learning system and you're trying to decide

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.800
whether or not to use end-to-end deep learning, I think the key question is,

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.240
do you have sufficient data to learn a function of the complexity needed to map

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.640
from X to Y?

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:49.440
I don't have a formal definition of this phrase, complexity needed, but intuitively,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.280
if you're trying to learn a function from X to Y that is looking at an image like

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.440
this and recognizing the position of the bones in this image, then maybe this

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.040
seems like a relatively simple problem to identify the bones in the image, and

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.920
maybe you don't need that much data for that task.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:16.580
So given a picture of a person, maybe finding the face of that person in the

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.620
image doesn't seem like that hard a problem, so maybe you don't need too much

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:25.060
data to find the face of a person, or at least maybe you can find an update

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.260
to solve that task.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:32.260
Whereas in contrast, the function needed to look at a hand and map that

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:36.540
directly to the age of a child, that seems like a much more complex

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.500
problem that intuitively maybe you need more data to learn if you were to

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.860
apply a pure end-to-end deep learning approach.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.180
So let me finish this video with a more complex example.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.460
You might know that I've been spending time helping out an autonomous

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:58.260
driving company, Drive.ai, so I'm actually very excited about autonomous

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.180
driving.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.020
So how do you build a car that drives itself?

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.900
Well, here's one thing you could do, and this is not an end-to-end deep

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:08.980
learning approach.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.940
You can take this input, an image of what's in front of your car, maybe

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:18.420
radar, LiDAR, other sensor readings as well, but to simplify the

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:23.460
description, let's just say you take a picture of what's in front of what's

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.660
around your car, and then to drive your car safely, you need to detect other

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:33.940
cars, and you also need to detect pedestrians.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.740
You need to detect other things, of course, but we'll just present a simplified

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.660
example here.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.020
Having figured out where are the other cars and pedestrians, you then need to

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:49.190
plan your own route.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:55.770
So in other words, if you see where the other cars were, the pedestrians,

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:59.690
you need to decide how to steer your own car, what path to steer your own

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.210
car for the next several seconds.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:09.610
And then having decided that you're going to drive a certain path, maybe if

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:15.970
this is a top-down view of a road, that's your car, maybe you've decided to

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:22.890
drive that path, that's what the route is, then you need to execute this

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:27.210
by generating the appropriate steering as well as the acceleration and breaking

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.010
commands.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:33.090
So in going from your image or your sensor impulse to detecting cars and

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.730
pedestrians, that can be done pretty well using deep learning.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.890
But then having figured out where the other cars and pedestrians are going

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:45.970
to select this route to exactly how you want to move your car, usually that's

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:47.450
not done with deep learning.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:52.050
Instead, that's done with a piece of software called motion planning.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.610
And if you ever take a class in robotics, you learn about motion

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:55.810
planning.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:58.930
And then having decided what's the path you want to steer your car through,

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:03.170
there'll be some other algorithm, I'm going to say it's a control algorithm,

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:08.730
that then generates the exact decision that then decides exactly how much to

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:12.210
turn the steering wheel and how much to step on the accelerator or step on

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.450
the brake.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:18.170
So I think what this example illustrates is that you want to use

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:27.360
machine learning or use deep learning to learn some individual components.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:35.230
And when applying supervised learning, you should carefully choose what

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:45.220
types of XY mappings you want to learn, depending on what task you can get

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:49.220
data for.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:53.060
And in contrast, it is exciting to talk about a pure end to end deep

00:08:53.060 --> 00:09:02.700
learning approach where you input an image and directly output a steering.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.820
But given data availability and the types of things we can learn with

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:12.380
neural networks today, this is actually not the most promising approach.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:18.860
Or this is not an approach that I think teams have gotten to work best.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.460
And I think this is actually this pure end to end deep learning approach is

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:27.140
actually less promising than most sophisticated approaches like this,

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:32.460
given the availability of data and our ability to train neural networks today.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.060
So that's it for end to end deep learning.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:39.860
It can sometimes work really well, but you also have to be mindful of where you

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:42.860
apply end to end deep learning.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:47.020
Finally, thank you and congrats on making it this far with me.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.780
If you finished last week's videos and this week's videos, then I think you'll

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:55.220
already be much smarter and much more strategic and much more able to make

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.420
good prioritization decisions in terms of how to move forward on your

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:03.540
machine learning project, even compared to a lot of machine learning engineers

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.420
and researchers that I see here in Silicon Valley.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.900
So congrats on all that you've learned so far.

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:12.980
And I hope you now also take a look at this week's homework problems, which

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:16.620
will give you another opportunity to practice these ideas and make sure

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:17.620
that you're mastering them.
